2. REMOVE THE FIXED 420x420 RIG WORLD
==================================================

Current RIG does this:

    <RigViewport
      ...
      size={420}
    />

That is wrong for the desired layout.

The 3D world must NOT be a fixed square.

RIG viewport should fill all available space between the Forge tab/header and
the right sidebar:

    width: 100% of remaining centre area
    height: 100%
    flex: 1
    minWidth: 0
    minHeight: 0

Use a ResizeObserver exactly as SCRIPT does.

The canvas must resize in-place when:

    window changes
    right panel opens/closes
    Forge panel changes size
    browser zoom/layout changes

Do NOT rebuild the WebGL renderer just because dimensions change.

==================================================
3. USE THE SAME WORLD SPACE AS SCRIPT
==================================================

RIG must use the same WORLD conventions as SCRIPT.

That means the same:

- Three.js coordinate system
- camera FOV
- near/far planes
- orbit target conventions
- camera orbit behaviour
- pan behaviour if SCRIPT supports it
- wheel zoom/dolly behaviour
- grid size/orientation
- grid theme colours
- background treatment
- renderer antialiasing
- devicePixelRatio treatment
- lighting defaults
- scene unit scale

Do NOT hand-copy approximate values if there is already a shared helper.

Prefer extracting/reusing the SCRIPT viewport implementation.

The goal is:

    if an object at world position X,Y,Z is shown in SCRIPT
    and the same object is shown in RIG,
    it appears in the SAME world coordinate system.

No RIG-only scale illusion.

No fixed 420px framing.

No separate camera convention.

==================================================
4. PREFERRED ARCHITECTURE
==================================================

Do NOT keep two independently maintained viewport engines:

    usePreviewViewport()
    RigViewport()

That guarantees drift.

Instead extract/shared-build a generic workspace viewport, for example:

    useWorkspaceViewport(...)
or
    SharedForgeViewport

which owns:

    scene
    camera
    renderer
    grid
    orbit controls
    resize
    theme
    lifecycle/disposal

Then supply MODE-SPECIFIC scene population.

SCRIPT population:

    synthetic SCRIPT slot objects
    optional bounds box

RIG population:

    rig hierarchy groups
    MODJOIN parent hierarchy
    MODPOS local offsets
    per-part animation

The world/camera implementation should be ONE thing.

If refactoring usePreviewViewport safely is too large for this pass, then make
RIG call/usePreviewViewport-compatible shared internals rather than recreating
camera/render behaviour a third time.

